a) Given 920 TB cases in 2024 and a cumulative 1020 HIV cases since 1993, what does this imply about the country''s infectious disease burden? (2 marks)', 'a) TB remains a major public health problem: 920 cases in one year is a high burden for a population of about 0.78 million (over 100 per 100 000) and the rising notifications show ongoing transmission (and better detection). HIV is a low-prevalence epidemic, but 1020 cumulative cases and 85 new cases a year show continuing transmission. Together they show a persistent communicable disease burden alongside the growing NCD burden, needing sustained investment.', null, '[{"point": "TB burden high and rising (920 in one year in a small population), continuing transmission; HIV low prevalence but steady new cases (85/yr), concentrated; dual burden alongside NCDs, need sustained programmes", "marks": 2}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
