  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case I: Leptospirosis outbreak in Dewathang
On 1 July 2025, the Ministry of Health issued a press release stating that "Leptospirosis Confirmed as Cause of Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) Cluster in Dewathang", based on laboratory confirmation and epidemiological links.
The WHO South-East Asia Region Epidemiological Bulletin (13th edition, 2 July 2025) reported that of 230 trainees, 143 developed symptoms and were treated according to clinical protocols. Of these, three died. The Ministry has started outbreak investigation and rapid response.

e) Define emerging and re-emerging infectious diseases with suitable examples. Describe what you understand by the One Health approach to disease prevention and control. (10 marks)', 'Emerging infectious diseases are infections that have newly appeared in a population, or existed before but are rapidly increasing in incidence or geographic range - e.g. COVID-19, avian influenza (H5N1), Nipah virus, Zika, MERS. Re-emerging diseases are ones that were previously controlled or declining but are increasing again - e.g. tuberculosis (including MDR-TB), dengue, malaria in some areas, measles, cholera, and leptospirosis and scrub typhus in Bhutan. Drivers include climate change, urbanisation, travel, deforestation, antimicrobial resistance and weak health systems. One Health is a collaborative, multisectoral and transdisciplinary approach that recognises that human, animal and environmental health are interconnected; human, veterinary, environmental and agricultural sectors work together on joint surveillance, information sharing, preparedness and response for zoonoses (rabies, avian influenza, leptospirosis, anthrax), antimicrobial resistance and food safety. In Bhutan, joint investigations by the Ministry of Health and the Department of Livestock (e.g. rabies and leptospirosis control) are examples.', null, '[{"point": "Emerging: newly appearing in population or rapidly increasing incidence/ range - examples (COVID-19, Nipah, Zika, avian influenza H5N1/H7N9, MERS)", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Re-emerging: previously controlled diseases returning - examples (TB/ MDR-TB, dengue, malaria, measles, cholera, leptospirosis, scrub typhus)", "marks": 3}, {"point": "One Health: collaborative multisectoral approach recognising interconnection of human, animal and environmental health; zoonoses, AMR, food safety; joint surveillance, preparedness; Bhutan example (rabies, avian influenza, leptospirosis)", "marks": 4}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'public-health'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 10,
    1080, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (Technical, Public Health): BCSE 2025, batch 1", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2025 Main Examination, Technical Paper III, Public Health", "year": 2025, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/technical-paper-iii/"}}'::jsonb, 'me-technical-3-ph2025-case1', 6, null, null, (select id from public.specialisations where code = 'public-health'), null)
