  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case II: Non-communicable diseases
According to the World Health Organization (WHO), non-communicable diseases (NCDs) killed at least 43 million people in 2021, equivalent to 75% of non-pandemic-related deaths globally. In 2021, 18 million people died from an NCD before age 70. Of all NCD deaths, 73% are in low- and middle-income countries. Cardiovascular diseases caused at least 19 million deaths in 2021, followed by cancers (10 million), chronic respiratory diseases (COPD, 4 million) and diabetes (over 2 million, including kidney disease deaths caused by diabetes). These four groups account for 80% of all premature NCD deaths. The top causes of death in Bhutan in 2021 according to WHO are shown in Figure 1 (deaths per 100 000: ischaemic heart disease 64.6, COPD 47.9, stroke 41.1, tuberculosis 26.6, cirrhosis of the liver 19.4, diarrhoeal diseases 18.9, diabetes 18.7, lower respiratory infections 15, stomach cancer 13.1, kidney diseases 12.8).

a) Describe the behavioural, metabolic and environmental risk factors of the major NCDs (CVD, COPD, liver cirrhosis, diabetes and cancers). (10 marks)', 'Behavioural (modifiable) risk factors: tobacco use (smoking and chewing - CVD, COPD, many cancers); harmful use of alcohol (liver cirrhosis - a leading cause of death in Bhutan - cancers, hypertension); unhealthy diet high in salt (suja, ezay), sugar and fat and low in fruit and vegetables (CVD, diabetes, stomach and other cancers); physical inactivity (CVD, diabetes, obesity). Doma (betel nut) chewing is linked to oral cancers. Metabolic risk factors: raised blood pressure (the leading risk factor for CVD and stroke), overweight and obesity, raised blood glucose (diabetes), and raised blood lipids (cholesterol). Environmental risk factors: household air pollution from burning wood and biomass for cooking and heating (COPD, lung disease), outdoor air pollution, occupational exposures (dust, chemicals), and infections linked to cancers and liver disease (hepatitis B and C - liver cancer and cirrhosis, H. pylori - stomach cancer, HPV - cervical cancer); together with social determinants such as urbanisation, income and education.', null, '[{"point": "Behavioural: tobacco use, harmful alcohol (cirrhosis, cancers, CVD), unhealthy diet (salt, sugar, fat, low fruit/veg; doma), physical inactivity", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Metabolic: raised BP, overweight/ obesity, raised blood glucose, raised lipids", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Environmental: air pollution (indoor biomass smoke, outdoor PM), occupational exposures, urbanisation, infections (HBV/HCV, H. pylori, HPV); linked to each disease", "marks": 3}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'public-health'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 10,
    1080, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (Technical, Public Health): BCSE 2025, batch 1", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2025 Main Examination, Technical Paper III, Public Health", "year": 2025, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/technical-paper-iii/"}}'::jsonb, 'me-technical-3-ph2025-case2', 1, null, null, (select id from public.specialisations where code = 'public-health'), '/papers/me/tech3-ph-2025-causes-of-death.png')
  returning id
)
