  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case II: Non-communicable diseases
According to the World Health Organization (WHO), non-communicable diseases (NCDs) killed at least 43 million people in 2021, equivalent to 75% of non-pandemic-related deaths globally. In 2021, 18 million people died from an NCD before age 70. Of all NCD deaths, 73% are in low- and middle-income countries. Cardiovascular diseases caused at least 19 million deaths in 2021, followed by cancers (10 million), chronic respiratory diseases (COPD, 4 million) and diabetes (over 2 million, including kidney disease deaths caused by diabetes). These four groups account for 80% of all premature NCD deaths. The top causes of death in Bhutan in 2021 according to WHO are shown in Figure 1 (deaths per 100 000: ischaemic heart disease 64.6, COPD 47.9, stroke 41.1, tuberculosis 26.6, cirrhosis of the liver 19.4, diarrhoeal diseases 18.9, diabetes 18.7, lower respiratory infections 15, stomach cancer 13.1, kidney diseases 12.8).

b) How do NCDs affect socio-economic development and achievement of the 2030 Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)? (10 marks)', 'NCDs reduce development in several ways. Economic: they kill and disable people in their productive years, reducing the workforce, productivity and household income; treatment is long-term and costly (dialysis, cancer care, referrals to India), increasing government health spending in Bhutan''s free health system and diverting resources from other sectors; households face catastrophic and out-of-pocket costs (travel, indirect costs). Social: families fall into poverty (a poverty trap), children may leave school to care for sick parents, inequalities widen because the poor have more risk and less access, and well-being and GNH are reduced. SDGs: NCDs directly threaten SDG target 3.4 (reduce premature NCD mortality by one-third by 2030 and promote mental health), 3.5 (alcohol), 3.a (tobacco control) and 3.8 (UHC); and indirectly SDG 1 (no poverty), SDG 8 (decent work and economic growth), SDG 10 (reduced inequalities) and SDG 11 (healthy cities, air quality). Achieving the SDGs needs a whole-of-government, multisectoral NCD response.', null, '[{"point": "Economic: loss of productivity/ premature death of working-age, absenteeism, high treatment costs (referrals abroad), catastrophic expenditure, pressure on free health system budget", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Social: poverty trap, burden on families/ carers, inequality, reduced GNH/ well-being", "marks": 3}, {"point": "SDGs: target 3.4 (reduce premature NCD mortality by one-third), affects SDG 1 (poverty), 3.8 UHC, 8 (growth), 10 (inequality); need multisectoral action", "marks": 3}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'public-health'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 10,
