  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case II: Non-communicable diseases
According to the World Health Organization (WHO), non-communicable diseases (NCDs) killed at least 43 million people in 2021, equivalent to 75% of non-pandemic-related deaths globally. In 2021, 18 million people died from an NCD before age 70. Of all NCD deaths, 73% are in low- and middle-income countries. Cardiovascular diseases caused at least 19 million deaths in 2021, followed by cancers (10 million), chronic respiratory diseases (COPD, 4 million) and diabetes (over 2 million, including kidney disease deaths caused by diabetes). These four groups account for 80% of all premature NCD deaths. The top causes of death in Bhutan in 2021 according to WHO are shown in Figure 1 (deaths per 100 000: ischaemic heart disease 64.6, COPD 47.9, stroke 41.1, tuberculosis 26.6, cirrhosis of the liver 19.4, diarrhoeal diseases 18.9, diabetes 18.7, lower respiratory infections 15, stomach cancer 13.1, kidney diseases 12.8).

c) (ii) What epidemiological study designs can be used to evaluate a screening programme for type 2 diabetes and hypertension? Describe briefly. (6 marks)', '1. Randomised controlled trials (individual or cluster/community randomised): populations randomly allocated to screening or usual care and followed for outcomes such as cardiovascular events, complications and mortality - the strongest evidence, but costly and long. 2. Cohort studies: compare outcomes in people who were screened and those not screened over time (e.g. incidence of stroke or diabetic complications). 3. Case-control studies: compare the screening history of people with advanced disease or complications (cases) with those without (controls). 4. Ecological/time-trend studies: compare rates of complications or deaths before and after introduction of screening, or between districts with and without programmes. Evaluation must consider lead-time bias, length bias and volunteer (selection) bias, and also process indicators - coverage, yield of new cases, sensitivity/specificity and linkage to treatment and control rates (e.g. the PEN HEART programme in Bhutan).', null, '[{"point": "Randomised controlled trials (individual or cluster) comparing screened vs not screened on outcomes (mortality, complications) - gold standard", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Cohort studies (screened vs unscreened followed up), case-control studies (prior screening history in cases of complications/ death vs controls)", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Ecological/ time-trend studies; also process indicators and biases (lead time, length, selection/ volunteer bias) to consider", "marks": 2}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
