  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case II: Non-communicable diseases
According to the World Health Organization (WHO), non-communicable diseases (NCDs) killed at least 43 million people in 2021, equivalent to 75% of non-pandemic-related deaths globally. In 2021, 18 million people died from an NCD before age 70. Of all NCD deaths, 73% are in low- and middle-income countries. Cardiovascular diseases caused at least 19 million deaths in 2021, followed by cancers (10 million), chronic respiratory diseases (COPD, 4 million) and diabetes (over 2 million, including kidney disease deaths caused by diabetes). These four groups account for 80% of all premature NCD deaths. The top causes of death in Bhutan in 2021 according to WHO are shown in Figure 1 (deaths per 100 000: ischaemic heart disease 64.6, COPD 47.9, stroke 41.1, tuberculosis 26.6, cirrhosis of the liver 19.4, diarrhoeal diseases 18.9, diabetes 18.7, lower respiratory infections 15, stomach cancer 13.1, kidney diseases 12.8).

d) Describe the four levels of prevention of modern epidemics such as NCDs. Give examples of actions at each level that would be appropriate in a comprehensive programme to prevent cardiovascular diseases (ischaemic heart disease and stroke). (10 marks)', '1. Primordial prevention - preventing the emergence of risk factors in the population through social, economic and environmental policies: tobacco control (Tobacco Control Act, taxes), alcohol taxes and regulation, reducing salt and trans-fats in food, healthy school meals, urban planning with footpaths and parks, promoting traditional healthy diets from childhood. 2. Primary prevention - reducing risk factors in people before disease: health education and promotion on healthy diet (less salt and oil, more vegetables), regular physical activity, quitting tobacco and doma, limiting alcohol, weight control. 3. Secondary prevention - early detection and prompt treatment: screening adults for hypertension, diabetes and cholesterol (WHO PEN/HEARTS), total cardiovascular risk assessment, treatment with antihypertensives and statins, regular follow-up. 4. Tertiary prevention - reducing complications and disability after a heart attack or stroke: emergency care and thrombolysis, cardiac and stroke rehabilitation (physiotherapy), secondary prevention drugs (aspirin, statins, beta-blockers), and long-term support. (Quaternary prevention - avoiding overmedicalisation - may also be mentioned.)', null, '[{"point": "Primordial: prevent emergence of risk factors in population - policies (tobacco control law, salt reduction, healthy school meals, urban planning for activity), examples", "marks": 2.5}, {"point": "Primary: reduce risk factors in individuals/ population before disease - health promotion, diet, exercise, quit smoking, alcohol, BP/ cholesterol control", "marks": 2.5}, {"point": "Secondary: early detection and treatment - screening BP/ glucose/ lipids, PEN, treat hypertension, total CV risk", "marks": 2.5}, {"point": "Tertiary: reduce complications/ disability after disease - cardiac/ stroke care, rehabilitation, secondary prevention drugs (aspirin, statins)", "marks": 2.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'public-health'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 10,
    1080, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
